{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Report\n\nTwo agents measured the store write lock held ~12 minutes by a process that had ALREADY\nEXITED. Investigated under bug royal-weariless-zebrafish; two hypotheses were REFUTED there\n(not an fd leak — PEP 446 non-inheritable fds, no `os.fork()`, `close_fds=True`; and not\n`start=-` defeating liveness — a genuinely dead pid IS reclaimed).\n\n## Premise verified in current `origin/main` (cc69cc1179)\n\n`src/rebar/_store/lock.py`, `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` (line **507**), same-host/same-namespace\nbranch, lines **565-572**:\n\n```python\nif not _pid_alive(pid):\n    return True                                     # 565-566\nstamped_start = None if fields[\"start\"] == _STAMP_UNKNOWN else fields[\"start\"]   # 567\ncurrent_start = _process_start_time(pid)                                          # 568\nif stamped_start is not None and current_start is not None and stamped_start != current_start:\n    return True                                     # 569-571  -- recycled-pid discriminator\nreturn False                                        # 572\n```\n\n`_process_start_time` (line **265**) reads **only** `/proc/<pid>/stat` (line 273) and returns\n`None` on every `OSError` (lines 275-276). On macOS there is no `/proc`, so it ALWAYS returns\n`None`. Therefore `current_start` at line 568 is always `None` there, the line-569 condition\nis **unsatisfiable by construction**, and the branch falls to `return False`.\n\nWhen the original holder is dead but its pid NUMBER has been recycled by an unrelated live\nprocess, `_pid_alive` reports \"live\" and the lock is judged fresh. That branch is deliberately\nexempt from the 1h `_MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S` backstop (constant at line **107**; the\nexemption is documented in the decision table at lines **525-531**, \"never on a timer alone\"),\nbecause a live pid is treated as positive proof of liveness. With no start time it is not\nproof — it is a bare pid-number collision. Net effect on any platform without `/proc`: an\n**unbounded** wedge. Recycling is live on this host (`kern.maxproc: 16000`; the counter has\nalready wrapped past the observed stamp pid).\n\nSecondary, already-bounded wedge: `_reclaim_mkdir_lock` (line **575**) removes the owner file\n(line **596**) then rmdirs (line **600**), both best-effort. A failed rmdir leaves an\nowner-less dir, which the line-**562** owner-unreadable branch clears at the ceiling. This\nticket asserts that bound with a test; it does not change that code.\n\n## Direction (conservative, exactly as recorded)\n\nApply `_mkdir_lock_age_exceeds_ceiling` on the live-pid branch **only when `current_start` is\nunavailable** — i.e. only when the \"live pid\" verdict is unqualified and therefore not a real\nliveness proof:\n\n* `current_start` known and DIFFERENT from a known `stamped_start` -> reclaim now (unchanged).\n* `current_start` known and matching -> `False`, never reclaimed on a timer (unchanged;\n  preserves \"never break a live owner on a timer alone\" wherever `/proc` exists).\n* `current_start` is `None` -> the verdict is unqualified, so fall back to the same 1h ceiling\n  that already backstops every other unprovable case in this function. This is the ONLY\n  behaviour change.\n* `stamped_start` `None` with `current_start` known -> unchanged `False` (not widened).\n\n**Considered and deliberately NOT done:** also reclaiming on `fcntl_held` in the unqualified\ncase. `_acquire_mkdir` (line **602**) does pass `fcntl_held=True` and its docstring argues\nthat hold is positive proof of the stamped owner's death, so this would reclaim an orphan\nimmediately rather than after an hour. It is nonetheless a widening of *when a lock may be\nbroken* on a safety-critical path, beyond the recorded direction, so it is left out and noted\nhere for a follow-up decision.\n\n## Module-size seam (required, not optional)\n\n`src/rebar/_store/lock.py` is **798** lines against the hard cap of **800**\n(`.github/module-size-limit.txt`, enforced by the CI module-size gate). The fix cannot land\nwithout a split. Split along the existing call-graph seam: extract the owner-identity /\nstamp / staleness-adjudication / reclaim cluster into a new sibling\n`src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py` —\n\n`_STAMP_UNKNOWN`, `_STAMP_V2_PREFIX`, `_BOOT_ID_PATH`, `_PID_NS_PATH`, `_MKDIR_OWNER_FILE`,\n`_MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S`, `_read_boot_id`, `_read_pid_namespace_id`,\n`_process_start_time`, `_host_identity`, `_owner_stamp`, `_parse_v2_stamp`,\n`_describe_stamped_pid`, `_mkdir_lock_age_s`, `_mkdir_lock_age_exceeds_ceiling`, `_pid_alive`,\n`_legacy_stamp_is_stale`, `_mkdir_lock_is_stale`, `_reclaim_mkdir_lock`\n\n— these call each other and nothing above them in `lock.py`'s acquire path, leaving `lock.py`\nwith the fcntl leg, `_acquire_mkdir`, `LockHandle`, and the `acquire`/`write_lock` API. Both\nmodules land well inside the 200-800 band; no file is created under 100 LOC.\n\n**Load-bearing migration detail.** A reference sweep found 4 test modules and **12 string-form\n`monkeypatch.setattr` sites** that patch these names on the `rebar._store.lock` module object\n(`_read_boot_id`, `_read_pid_namespace_id`, `_process_start_time`, `_pid_alive`,\n`_parse_v2_stamp`), plus 2 sites patching `_lock.socket.gethostname` which `_host_identity`\nreads. A re-export shim in `lock.py` would silence the `AttributeError` but **silently break\nthe tests' intent**, because the moved `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` resolves these names through its\nOWN module globals. Every such site must be repointed at `lock_owner`, not shimmed. No\n`from rebar._store.lock import <private>` form exists anywhere, and no doc cites these by line.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A dead holder whose pid number has been recycled by an unrelated live process is\n      reclaimed once past the ceiling on a platform where `_process_start_time` returns\n      `None` — verified by a test that stamps a lock with a live-but-unrelated pid, forces no\n      start time, and asserts stale only past the ceiling (and NOT before it)\n- [ ] A live holder whose stamped and current start times both exist and MATCH is never\n      reclaimed, however old the lock — verified by a test asserting refusal well past the\n      ceiling\n- [ ] A live pid whose known start time DIFFERS from the stamp is still reclaimed immediately,\n      independent of age — verified by a test (regression guard on the existing branch)\n- [ ] The owner-less-dir path is bounded by the ceiling — verified by a test that removes the\n      owner file and asserts stale only past the ceiling\n- [ ] `lock.py` and the new `lock_owner.py` are each within the limit in\n      `.github/module-size-limit.txt`, and no new file is under 100 LOC — verified against\n      `git archive HEAD`\n- [ ] The split is behaviour-preserving: all pre-existing lock tests pass with their patch\n      targets repointed to `lock_owner`, with no re-export shim for the moved private names"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786558339714813001,"uuid":"14956a70-5107-4d87-b227-109c83cbdcfb"}